      "text": "## The Big Picture: The Cell Is a State Machine Under a Stress Budget"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-CELL-INJURY-ADAPTATION-AND-DEATH.md#007",
      "path": [
        "01-CELL-INJURY-ADAPTATION-AND-DEATH.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:pathology:01-cell-injury-adaptation-and-death]",
        "canonical_path": "pathology/01-CELL-INJURY-ADAPTATION-AND-DEATH.md",
        "concepts": "[reversible-irreversible-injury, hypoxia-oxidative-stress, cellular-adaptation, necrosis-apoptosis, accumulations-calcification]",
        "current_path": "pathology/01-CELL-INJURY-ADAPTATION-AND-DEATH.md",
        "id": "maxim:pathology:cell-injury-adaptation-and-death",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "pathology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[cell-injury]",
        "section": "pathology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Cell Injury, Adaptation, and Death",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The novice mental model is \"cells are either alive or dead.\" The expert model is a **state\nmachine with hysteresis**: a cell holds **homeostasis** inside a normal load envelope; when\ndemand or stress changes persistently it shifts to a new **adapted** steady state; when stress\nexceeds what adaptation can absorb it enters **reversible injury** (a recoverable degraded\nmode); and past a threshold it crosses into **irreversible injury** and **death**. The\ntransitions are governed by a small set of molecular switches, and — critically — the\nreversible→irreversible transition is **one-way**."
    },
    {
      "id": "01-CELL-INJURY-ADAPTATION-AND-DEATH.md#008",
      "path": [
        "01-CELL-INJURY-ADAPTATION-AND-DEATH.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:pathology:01-cell-injury-adaptation-and-death]",
        "canonical_path": "pathology/01-CELL-INJURY-ADAPTATION-AND-DEATH.md",
        "concepts": "[reversible-irreversible-injury, hypoxia-oxidative-stress, cellular-adaptation, necrosis-apoptosis, accumulations-calcification]",
        "current_path": "pathology/01-CELL-INJURY-ADAPTATION-AND-DEATH.md",
        "id": "maxim:pathology:cell-injury-adaptation-and-death",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "pathology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[cell-injury]",
        "section": "pathology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Cell Injury, Adaptation, and Death",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nTHE CELL-STRESS STATE MACHINE  (this guide owns the whole diagram)\n==================================================================\n                 increased / decreased / altered demand\n   NORMAL  <------------------------------------------->  ADAPTED\n   homeostasis      (hypertrophy, hyperplasia, atrophy,    new steady\n     |               metaplasia — reversible)              state\n     |  injurious stimulus (hypoxia, ROS, toxin, physical, immune, genetic)\n     v\n   REVERSIBLE INJURY   cell swelling, fatty change; organelles degraded\n     |   remove stimulus -> RECOVER  (back to normal)\n     |\n     |   stimulus persists / is severe -> cross the point of no return\n     v\n   IRREVERSIBLE INJURY   mitochondrial collapse + membrane failure + Ca flood\n     |\n     +-------------------------+-------------------------------+\n     v                         v                               v\n   NECROSIS                 APOPTOSIS                   OTHER REGULATED DEATH\n   (accidental, messy,      (programmed, tidy,          (necroptosis, pyroptosis,\n    inflammatory)            usually quiet if cleared)    ferroptosis)\n```"
    },
