        "section": "pathology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Cell Injury, Adaptation, and Death",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Two facts from this diagram drive the guide. First, **adaptation is not failure** — it is a\nsuccessful move to a new steady state, and most adaptations are reversible if the driving\nload is removed. Second, **death is not one thing** — the *mode* of death (necrosis vs\napoptosis vs the regulated-necrosis family), together with clearance efficiency and the\nimmunogenic context, determines whether the tissue inflames; apoptosis is usually\nimmunologically quiet when apoptotic bodies are cleared promptly, but failed clearance or\nimmunogenic apoptosis can provoke inflammation. That consequence is the hand-off to `02`."
    }
